      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In the early 1900s, the town hosted Rutenber, a pioneering brass‑era automobile company that later became Western Motor Company.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logansport%2C_Indiana",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Bendix Company built its first automobile, the Bendix, from 1908 to 1909, marking the town’s brief automotive boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logansport%2C_Indiana",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Logansport’s historic Michigan Road, a 19th‑century artery, still cuts through the town, echoing its early role as a transportation crossroads.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logansport%2C_Indiana",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Logansport Art Association, founded in 1911, has long nurtured local artists and hosted exhibitions in the town’s historic Main Street.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logansport%2C_Indiana",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Every summer, Logansport celebrates its railroad heritage with the Iron Horse Festival, a nod to the town’s once‑thriving rail industry.",
